## Photoperiod

@fig-photoperiod compares photoperiod at the weighted 10th and 90th percentiles with the weighted median, at the same clock time or phase. Each factor includes the photoperiod main smooth and its interaction with time. The other model terms cancel in the comparison. A factor above 1 indicates higher fitted melanopic EDI + 0.1 than at the median photoperiod. The median reference stays at 1; pointwise bands use the covariance of each contrast with that reference.


::: {#fig-photoperiod .cell layout-ncol="1"}

```{.r .cell-code}
show_figure("primary_clock_photoperiod")
show_figure("primary_phase_photoperiod")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Clock time](results/figures/primary_clock_photoperiod.png){#fig-photoperiod-1 fig-alt='Clock and phase photoperiod factors relative to the median, with pointwise uncertainty bands and a reference line at one. The diagonally striped vertical band in the circadian-phase panel marks the DLMO reference.' width=900}
:::

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Circadian phase](results/figures/primary_phase_photoperiod.png){#fig-photoperiod-2 fig-alt='Clock and phase photoperiod factors relative to the median, with pointwise uncertainty bands and a reference line at one. The diagonally striped vertical band in the circadian-phase panel marks the DLMO reference.' width=900}
:::

Photoperiod factors at the 10th, 50th and 90th percentiles of the common participant-weighted reference distribution, relative to its median. The diagonally striped vertical band in the circadian-phase panel marks the DLMO reference.
:::


## Fit, prediction and attribution

### In-sample fit

@tbl-fit reports the in-sample fit statistics. Adjusted R² is `mgcv`'s adjusted fit statistic. Deviance explained and ordinary R² describe in-sample fit; for these Gaussian models, ordinary R² is 1 minus residual sum of squares divided by the total sum of squares. They include fitted participant and day terms and do not measure prediction for new people.


::: {#tbl-fit .cell tbl-cap='In-sample fit statistics, kept separate from predictive performance and attribution.'}

```{.r .cell-code}
show_table(table_data("model_fit") |>
  select(model, axis, adjusted_R2, deviance_explained, ordinary_R2,
         participants, participant_days, half_hours))
```

::: {.cell-output-display}

