### Exposure patterns and changes in the group ratio

The higher phase-aligned exposure in the extreme evening group after MSFsc becomes more pronounced on longer days. For this exploratory assessment, we use 95% pointwise intervals on the 10-minute phase grid, based on the coefficient covariance with smoothing-parameter correction. Each interval addresses one phase-specific comparison; it does not provide simultaneous coverage over the complete curve. Interval-average comparisons use the covariance of the average log contrast and treat the two intervals identified by the primary 95% simultaneous bands within the phase coordinate as fixed. These comparisons are not adjusted for multiplicity or interval selection. 

@fig-daylight-patterns shows the scenario curves, @fig-daylight-ratios compares the two extreme groups, and @fig-daylight-moderation isolates the longer-versus-shorter-day change.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
show_figure("daylight_phase_patterns")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Phase curves at −1, 0 and +1 hours of site-centered photoperiod. Bands are pointwise 95% intervals. Facet counts describe observed participants and days within one hour of the target; the full model uses 140 participants and 778 days. Grey windows with dashed boundaries mark the two primary phase intervals; the diagonally striped band marks the DLMO reference.](results/figures/daylight_phase_patterns.png){#fig-daylight-patterns fig-alt='Three panels show extreme morning and evening phase curves on shorter, typical and longer days. The evening curve rises earlier after MSFsc in all panels. Each panel gives separate participant and day counts for both groups. Grey windows with dashed boundaries mark the two primary phase intervals; the diagonally striped band marks the DLMO reference.' width=1260}
:::
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
show_figure("daylight_phase_contrasts")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Extreme-evening/extreme-morning ratios of melanopic EDI plus 0.1 at the three targets. Bands are 95% pointwise intervals for each phase-specific group ratio; bold segments exclude one and meet the four-person support rule. Grey windows with dashed boundaries mark the two primary phase intervals; the diagonally striped band marks the DLMO reference.](results/figures/daylight_phase_contrasts.png){#fig-daylight-ratios fig-alt='The evening-to-morning ratio falls below one before MSFsc and rises above one after it. The later peak is higher in the longer-day scenario, with wide uncertainty bands. Bold sections mark supported pointwise intervals excluding one. Grey windows with dashed boundaries mark the two primary phase intervals; the diagonally striped band marks the DLMO reference.' width=1260}
:::
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
show_figure("daylight_phase_moderation")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Change in the extreme-group ratio from shorter to longer days. A value of one means the group ratio is unchanged. Bands are 95% pointwise intervals; bold sections exclude one and meet the four-person support rule. Grey windows with dashed boundaries mark the two primary phase intervals; the diagonally striped band marks the DLMO reference.](results/figures/daylight_phase_moderation.png){#fig-daylight-moderation fig-alt='The longer-to-shorter ratio of group ratios dips before MSFsc and peaks around two hours after MSFsc. Bold sections show where supported pointwise intervals exclude one. Grey windows with dashed boundaries mark the two primary phase intervals; the diagonally striped band marks the DLMO reference.' width=990}
:::
:::


Over the primary candidate interval from **0.92 to 4.42 hours after MSFsc**, the geometric mean evening/morning ratio is **7.99** at the −1-hour target and **20.69** at the +1-hour target. Their ratio is **2.59** (95% CI **1.06–6.34**), equivalent to an increase of **159.1%** (95% CI **5.9%–534.2%**). Over the earlier interval, **11.25 to 9.58 hours before MSFsc**, the corresponding change is **−16.6%** (95% CI **−66.6% to +108.5%**). The later increase is supported by this exploratory interval-average comparison, while the earlier point estimate is in the proposed direction. The bold pointwise phase comparisons exclude no change from approximately **1.17 to 3.17 hours after MSFsc**. These intervals were selected in the primary analysis and are reused here with no selection or multiplicity adjustment.

Before the DLMO reference interval, the largest estimated decrease in the group ratio occurs around **8.33 hours before MSFsc**: the longer-day evening/morning ratio is **0.41 times** the shorter-day ratio (pointwise 95% CI **0.16–1.04**). This indication of a photoperiod effect lies closer to DLMO than the primary exposure difference at **9.6–11.3 hours before MSFsc**. Its interval still includes no change; the pointwise exclusions of one occur after MSFsc.

@tbl-daylight-primary-intervals summarizes the scenario ratios within the two primary candidate intervals.


::: {#tbl-daylight-primary-intervals .cell tbl-cap='Scenario ratios within the two primary candidate phase intervals. Bounds are 95% conditional Gaussian intervals for each average log contrast, with the selected interval treated as fixed and no multiplicity or selection adjustment. Counts include actual observed phase midpoints within each reported interval and within one hour of the target. Each sample entry gives participants / participant-days / valid half-hours.'}

```{.r .cell-code}
daylight_interval_counts <- table_data("daylight_primary_interval_samples") |>
  filter(contrast != "Longer_minus_shorter") |>
  mutate(sample = paste(participants, participant_days, half_hours, sep = " / ")) |>
  select(interval, contrast, quartile, sample) |>
  pivot_wider(names_from = quartile, values_from = sample)
show_table(table_data("daylight_primary_interval_summaries") |>
  filter(contrast != "Longer_minus_shorter") |>
  select(interval, start_phase, end_phase, contrast, ratio, ratio_lower, ratio_upper) |>
  left_join(daylight_interval_counts, by = c("interval", "contrast")))
```

