The [night-level registry](results/data/sleep_diary_nights.csv) retains inclusion decisions for all imported entries. The [participant SD table](results/tables/sleep_diary_participant_sd.csv), [correlation table](results/tables/sleep_diary_correlations.csv), [participant-level correction inputs and results](results/tables/sleep_diary_msfsc_participants.csv), [corrected-timing summary](results/tables/sleep_diary_msfsc_summary.csv), [exact scatterplot data for all three panels](data/intermediate/sleep_diary_scatter_data.csv), [marginal histogram counts](data/intermediate/sleep_diary_scatter_histogram_data.csv), [plotted correlation labels](data/intermediate/sleep_diary_scatter_correlations_data.csv), [input checksums](results/provenance/sleep_diary_inputs.csv) and [runtime record](results/provenance/sleep_diary_runtime.txt) support reproduction. Running `Rscript --vanilla R/sleep_diary_alignment.R` executes the same stages independently; the report executes them here in analysis order.

### Exposure-analysis sensitivity using MSF~sc(diary)~

The separate [MSF~sc(diary)~ sensitivity analysis](diary-msfsc-sensitivity.qmd) repeats the exposure models and downstream calculations using this diary-derived correlate. It compares the established questionnaire-based MSFsc measure with MSF~sc(diary)~ on the same 135 participants, 755 participant-days and 34,884 half-hours. Both pre- and post-midsleep exposure contrasts retain their directions. However, within the original post-MSFsc window, the longer-day/shorter-day ratio is **2.66 (95% CI 1.10–6.43)** with questionnaire MSFsc and **1.15 (0.52–2.54)** with MSF~sc(diary)~. Daylength moderation is significant in the primary questionnaire-based analysis and its matched repeat, but not in the diary-derived sensitivity. An additional comparison uses diary timing while retaining questionnaire quartiles: its post-MSFsc factor is **2.39 (0.95–6.02)**. Alignment alone therefore leaves the point estimate similar but its interval includes one; reclassification produces the larger reduction in the estimate. The separate document reports this [fixed-group comparison](diary-msfsc-sensitivity.qmd#sec-fixed-groups) and the full matched-sample analyses, including PRC and Emens summaries, prediction, attribution and model-structure checks.


## Comparison with Goulet and colleagues {#sec-comparison-goulet}

### The question shared by the two studies

@goulet2007 explored whether morning and evening types encounter different light patterns when exposure is expressed in clock time or relative to measured circadian phase. That distinction also motivates this analysis. In both studies, the phase comparison tests whether a clock-time difference reflects a shift of the whole daily pattern or a difference that remains after alignment.

The earlier study analysed nine morning and ten evening participants after data-quality exclusions. In the full sample, morning types received more light in the morning and evening types more light late at night. Aligning the observations to measured DLMO removed a statistically detectable group-by-time difference in that full sample. The important exception was the subset with extreme DLMO timing: four morning and five evening participants. In that subset, morning types had higher exposure during much of the three hours before to three hours after DLMO, and evening types had higher exposure 10–12 hours after DLMO. The intermediate-DLMO subset did not show a statistically detectable group-by-time interaction [@goulet2007, pp. 153–156].

“Intermediate” and “extreme” in that comparison described DLMO timing within participants already selected as morning or evening types by MEQ. They were not MEQ severity categories or MSFsc quartiles. The two studies therefore address related questions with different group definitions.

### Design differences and what they contribute

@tbl-study-comparison shows which differences strengthen the present analysis and which limit direct comparison.

The sensor-placement comparison uses the context-specific errors in momentary exposure reported from concurrent MeLiDos recordings by @zauner2026placement.


